# KS-1188 (F1b only) — Wednesday's task for Ornith, VITEST, **TEST-ONLY MODE, ONE NEW TEST FILE** (written 06:09 on 2026-09-17)

Written from origin develop `e0f41a8fafd64fa31524390cfeab320e822f3d15` (#1013's squash, KS-999); develop then moved to
`523f283c6cd2550263ec9869dc5ee722be40df4e` (#1011, KS-871, api-gateway only; the `services/auth` and `packages/shared` trees
are identical at both), and the input pins `523f283c6`. The product file
`Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/routes/users.ts` (1355 lines, blob `89da72df7`) was read at `:1-30`, `:840-916`, `:1129`
and for every route registration. `userRepo.ts` `getUserById` (`:407-419`), `fromRow` (`:218-255`), `dbErrors.ts` whole and
`errorHandler.ts` (`:75-81`, `:251-318`) were read. The reference test `ks1013-admin-patch-malformed-id.test.ts` (186 lines)
was read whole; this task copies its mount shape (the REAL `userRoutes` and the REAL `errorHandler` on `127.0.0.1:0`, the
`authenticate` stand-in, the `services/session` mock) but NOT its `userRepo` mock: here `userRepo` is REAL. The #1013 gate
verdict, its HTTP probe and its tamper table were read. **PRE-MEASURED** in a scratch clone at the tip; see
`## Premises (measured)`. **This is a PARTIAL of KS-1188:** F1a (`GET /api/auth/mfa/status`, product `routes/mfa.ts`) is its
own task; F2 (the log meta keys) is already held; F3 (the backup-code burn order) is a product decision.

## THE MODE — read this twice

TEST-ONLY, ONE NEW FILE. Your diff contains EXACTLY ONE file: the NEW test file
`Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/__tests__/ks1188-users-me-503-route.test.ts`, written as
`--- /dev/null` then `+++ b/…`, one `@@ -0,0 +1,144 @@` hunk, every line `+`.
**Do NOT modify `routes/users.ts`**, and do NOT modify `ks1013-admin-patch-malformed-id.test.ts` (the reference test),
`userRepo.ts` or `errorHandler.ts`. `users.ts` is in files[...] only so you can see the handler the new test reaches. The
checker plants the tamper named at the bottom into a clone of `users.ts` and expects the three 🔴 cells RED and the two 🟢
controls GREEN, then expects all five GREEN at the untouched tip.

## What is wrong (one paragraph)

KS-999 (#1013) made `getUserById` await `fromRow` (`userRepo.ts:410`), so a subject-DEK read failure inside `fromRow` now
reaches its catch, which rethrows an infrastructure failure as `ServiceUnavailableError` (503) and anything else as-is
(500 through the error handler). The #1013 gate measured `GET /api/users/me` (`meHandler`, `users.ts:850-913`) answering 503
with the fixed body `{"success":false,"error":{"code":"SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE","message":"Authentication service temporarily unavailable, please retry"}}`
under a DEK infrastructure fault, but no cell drives that route under the fault. Re-measured at the tip: if `meHandler`'s
catch (`:909`) rewrote a 503 as a plain Error (so the route answered 500 INTERNAL_ERROR, the KS-253 regression shape), 0 of
745 auth cells went red. **This task:** a NEW file mounts the REAL `userRoutes`, the REAL `userRepo` and the REAL
`errorHandler` on loopback, rejects `getDek` for the row, and asserts the route answers 503 with the fixed body on the
message-form, pg `53300` and `ECONNREFUSED` faults; a non-infrastructure DEK failure still answers 500.

## Where (no product site changes — this list is informational; every line below STAYS)

- `:850` — (correct) `export const meHandler = async (req: AuthenticatedRequest, res: Response, next: import('express').NextFunction) => {` — the handler under test. Stays.
- `:852` — (correct) `    const user = await userRepo.getUserById(req.user!.userId);` — the read whose 503 the cells pin. Stays.
- `:853` — (correct) `    if (!user) throw new NotFoundError('User');` — why a swallowed read would answer 404, not 503. Stays.
- `:909` — (correct) `    next(error);` — the catch hands the error to the REAL errorHandler. The tamper replaces this line. Stays.
- `:913` — (correct) `userRoutes.get('/me', authenticate(), meHandler);` — the mount the cells call. Stays.

## The test — CREATE THE NEW FILE `Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/__tests__/ks1188-users-me-503-route.test.ts`

**Harness:** `vitest run` inside `Blockchain/Dev/services/auth` (`package.json` scripts.test is `vitest`;
`vitest.config.ts`: globals, node environment, `setupFiles: ['./vitest.setup.ts']`). To run this file alone:
`npx vitest run src/__tests__/ks1188-users-me-503-route.test.ts`.

The mount (`express()`, `express.json()`, `app.use('/api/users', userRoutes)`, `app.use(errorHandler)`, `listen(0,
'127.0.0.1')`) and the `authenticate` and `services/session` mocks follow the ks1013 reference test. Unlike ks1013, `userRepo`
is NOT mocked: the `@secuura/shared` and `subjectDeks` mocks make any value that starts with `sdek:` subject-DEK
ciphertext, so the REAL `fromRow` reads the DEK for the row the `db.query` mock returns. `PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL` is removed
for the file (restored after), so the healthy handler never opens a platform pool. The logger is captured. No network
beyond `127.0.0.1`, no database.

**Reproduce the body below EXACTLY as written.** It is 144 lines. Do not shorten it, do not replace any part with a
comment, do not invent a helper, do not rename a variable, do not collapse the cells into a loop or an `it.each`. Every
name a cell reads (`USER_ID`, `UNAVAILABLE`, `INTERNAL`, `getDek`, `logError`, `getMe`, `errorLines`) is declared ABOVE the
first cell. **The file contains no backslash anywhere and no template literal; keep it that way.**

```ts
// KS-1188 F1b (the #1013 gate on KS-999): GET /api/users/me answers 503 when getUserById hits a DEK-read infrastructure
// fault, but no cell pinned the route-level status: the gate's Q-EH-503-AS-500 reddened 0 cells in ks999/ks949. These cells
// mount the REAL user routes, the REAL userRepo and the REAL errorHandler on 127.0.0.1:0, aim a getDek rejection at the row,
// and pin the status and the body of GET /api/users/me, so a meHandler catch that rewrote the 503 would go red.
import { describe, it, expect, beforeAll, afterAll, beforeEach, vi } from 'vitest';
import type { AddressInfo } from 'node:net';
import type { Server } from 'node:http';
import express from 'express';

vi.mock('@secuura/shared', async (importOriginal) => {
  const actual = await importOriginal<Record<string, unknown>>();
  return {
    ...actual,
    encryptField: (v: string) => v,
    decryptField: (v: string) => v,
    encryptFieldWithDek: (v: string) => v,
    decryptFieldWithDek: (v: string) => v,
    isSubjectDekCiphertext: (v: unknown) => typeof v === 'string' && v.startsWith('sdek:'),
    isEncryptedPii: () => false,
    lookupHash: (v: string) => 'hash:' + v,
  };
});

vi.mock('../services/subjectDeks', () => ({
  subjectDeks: {
    getDek: vi.fn(async () => Buffer.from('ks1188-throwaway-dek')),
    getOrCreateDek: vi.fn(async () => Buffer.from('ks1188-throwaway-dek')),
  },
}));

vi.mock('../utils/logger', () => ({
  logger: { info: vi.fn(), warn: vi.fn(), error: vi.fn(), debug: vi.fn() },
}));

vi.mock('../db', () => ({
  isDbAvailable: () => true,
  query: vi.fn(async (sql: string) => {
    if (String(sql).includes('SELECT') && String(sql).includes('FROM users')) {
      return { rows: [{ id: 'ks1188-users-me-user', email: 'sdek:ciphertext', status: 'active', role: 'user', mfa_enabled: true, created_at: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z', updated_at: '2026-01-01T00:00:00.000Z' }], rowCount: 1 };
    }
    return { rows: [], rowCount: 0 };
  }),
}));

vi.mock('../middleware/authenticate', () => {
  const inject = () => (req: any, _res: any, next: () => void) => {
    req.user = { userId: 'ks1188-users-me-user', role: 'user' };
    next();
  };
  return { authenticate: inject, authenticateAccessOrConnector: inject };
});

vi.mock('../services/session', () => ({ revokeAllUserSessions: vi.fn(async () => 0) }));

const USER_ID = 'ks1188-users-me-user';
const UNAVAILABLE = { success: false, error: { code: 'SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE', message: 'Authentication service temporarily unavailable, please retry' } };
const INTERNAL = { success: false, error: { code: 'INTERNAL_ERROR', message: 'An unexpected error occurred' } };

let server: Server;
let base = '';
let getDek: any;
let logError: any;
const savedPlatformUrl = process.env.PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL;

beforeAll(async () => {
  delete process.env.PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL;
  const { userRoutes } = await import('../routes/users');
  const { errorHandler } = await import('../middleware/errorHandler');
  getDek = (await import('../services/subjectDeks')).subjectDeks.getDek;
  logError = (await import('../utils/logger')).logger.error;
  const app = express();
  app.use(express.json());
  app.use('/api/users', userRoutes);
  app.use(errorHandler);
  await new Promise<void>((resolve) => {
    server = app.listen(0, '127.0.0.1', () => {
      base = 'http://127.0.0.1:' + String((server.address() as AddressInfo).port);
      resolve();
    });
  });
});

afterAll(async () => {
  await new Promise<void>((resolve) => server.close(() => resolve()));
  if (savedPlatformUrl !== undefined) process.env.PLATFORM_DATABASE_URL = savedPlatformUrl;
});

beforeEach(() => { vi.clearAllMocks(); });

/** GET /api/users/me over loopback: the status and the parsed JSON body. */
async function getMe(): Promise<{ status: number; body: any }> {
  const res = await fetch(base + '/api/users/me');
  return { status: res.status, body: await res.json() };
}

/** The messages the logger received at error level, in call order. */
function errorLines(): unknown[] {
  return logError.mock.calls.map((call: unknown[]) => call[0]);
}

describe('KS-1188 F1b — GET /api/users/me answers 503 on a getUserById DEK infrastructure fault', () => {
  it('🔴 KS-1188 F1b — a message-form DEK infrastructure fault answers 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE with the fixed body', async () => {
    getDek.mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error('connection terminated unexpectedly'));
    const r = await getMe();
    expect(r.status, 'the users/me status under a DEK infrastructure fault').toBe(503);
    expect(r.body).toStrictEqual(UNAVAILABLE);
    expect(getDek).toHaveBeenCalledWith(USER_ID);
  });

  it('🔴 KS-1188 F1b — a pg 53300 DEK fault answers 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE with the fixed body', async () => {
    getDek.mockRejectedValueOnce(Object.assign(new Error('sorry, too many clients already'), { code: '53300' }));
    const r = await getMe();
    expect(r.status, 'the users/me status under a DEK infrastructure fault').toBe(503);
    expect(r.body).toStrictEqual(UNAVAILABLE);
  });

  it('🔴 KS-1188 F1b — an ECONNREFUSED DEK fault answers 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE and never a profile', async () => {
    getDek.mockRejectedValueOnce(Object.assign(new Error('connect ECONNREFUSED 127.0.0.1:5432'), { code: 'ECONNREFUSED' }));
    const r = await getMe();
    expect(r.status, 'the users/me status under a DEK infrastructure fault').toBe(503);
    expect(r.body.success).toBe(false);
    expect(r.body.data).toBeUndefined();
  });

  it('🟢 KS-1188 control — a NON-infrastructure DEK failure reaches the REAL errorHandler and answers 500 INTERNAL_ERROR', async () => {
    getDek.mockRejectedValueOnce(new Error('ks1188-not-an-infrastructure-fault'));
    const r = await getMe();
    expect(r.status).toBe(500);
    expect(r.body).toStrictEqual(INTERNAL);
    expect(getDek).toHaveBeenCalledWith(USER_ID);
    expect(errorLines()).toContain('DB getUserById failed');
    expect(errorLines()).toContain('Error occurred');
  });

  it('🟢 KS-1188 control — with nothing rejecting the REAL route reads the row and answers 200 with the profile', async () => {
    const r = await getMe();
    expect(r.status).toBe(200);
    expect(r.body.success).toBe(true);
    expect(r.body.data.id).toBe(USER_ID);
    expect(r.body.data.mfaEnabled).toBe(true);
    expect(getDek).toHaveBeenCalledWith(USER_ID);
    expect(errorLines().length).toBe(0);
  });
});
```

Why each cell is what it is:
- **🔴 message form** (`connection terminated unexpectedly`, no `code`), **🔴 pg 53300** and **🔴 ECONNREFUSED**: the three
  infrastructure forms the gate measured. Each rejects `getDek` once; `getUserById` classifies it as infrastructure and
  throws the 503; `meHandler`'s catch hands it to the REAL errorHandler. Each cell asserts the status is 503 first, then the
  body. Under the tamper (`users.ts:909` rewrites an error whose `statusCode` is 503 as a plain `Error`) the route answers
  500 INTERNAL_ERROR, so all three fail **by assertion** on the status line: `expected 500 to be 503`.
- **🟢 control 1 (hit witness, real errorHandler):** a NON-infrastructure DEK failure (`ks1188-not-an-infrastructure-fault`)
  is rethrown as-is and the REAL errorHandler answers `500 INTERNAL_ERROR` with its fixed text. It also asserts `getDek` was
  read for the fixture id, that `DB getUserById failed` was logged (the real `userRepo` catch ran) and that `Error occurred`
  was logged (the real errorHandler ran). Green under the tamper (that error has no `statusCode` 503, so the tampered catch
  passes it through unchanged). Red if the fault stops reaching `getUserById`'s catch.
- **🟢 control 2 (fixture, real route):** nothing rejects, the row resolves, and the REAL handler answers 200 with the
  fixture's `id` and `mfaEnabled: true`, and nothing is logged at error level. It proves the reds come from the rejection.

## Red cells

- 🔴 KS-1188 F1b — a message-form DEK infrastructure fault answers 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE with the fixed body
- 🔴 KS-1188 F1b — a pg 53300 DEK fault answers 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE with the fixed body
- 🔴 KS-1188 F1b — an ECONNREFUSED DEK fault answers 503 SERVICE_UNAVAILABLE and never a profile

## Tamper (test-only mode — the checker plants this in a clone to prove your cells can go red)

line: 909
from: `    next(error);`
to: `    next((error as any)?.statusCode === 503 ? new Error('ks1188 tamper: the 503 rewritten as a plain error') : error);`
statement_ok: `:909` is a whole statement ending in `;`; the next lines are `:910` `  }` (closes the catch) and `:911` `};` (closes meHandler), which are block ends, not a continuation of the call, so nothing after it can veto the replacement (measured: the three 🔴 cells red by assertion, P6).

## Output

Exactly ONE ```diff block containing ONLY the new test file: `--- /dev/null` with NO leading space, then
`+++ b/Blockchain/Dev/services/auth/src/__tests__/ks1188-users-me-503-route.test.ts`, then
`@@ -0,0 +1,144 @@`, then the 144 lines above, each prefixed with `+`. Blank lines are a bare `+`. No hunk for
`users.ts` or any other file.

## Premises (measured)

